 <section class="three-tool-section" id="widget-deep-setup"><h3>How to embed a free prayer-time timetable on a website</h3><ol><li>Search for your city or supply verified latitude and longitude.</li><li>Check the IANA timezone such as Europe/London; daylight-saving changes follow that timezone.</li><li>Match the Fajr/Isha method and Asr convention to your local mosque or qualified authority.</li><li>Pick light or dark colours, a regular or compact display, and whether to include sunrise.</li><li>Review the live preview, copy the iframe HTML and paste it into a page that supports custom HTML.</li><li>Test mobile widths and check the times for at least one winter and summer day.</li></ol><p>The embed is hosted by sunnah.tools. Your website needs permission to render external iframes, and visitors need an internet connection to load it. The embed page is intentionally marked <code>noindex</code> to avoid duplicating standalone tool pages.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="widget-deep-times"><h3>What exactly do the five daily prayer times mean?</h3><p>The widget lists Fajr, Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib and Isha as calculated prayer <em>start</em> times. Sunrise is an astronomical reference time, not a sixth obligatory prayer. Congregation time (iqamah), the Adhan at a particular mosque, and prayer windows are not identical to a calculated start. <a href="https://quran.com/4/103">Quran 4:103</a> refers to Salah at appointed times.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="widget-deep-methods"><h3>Why do Fajr and Isha times differ by calculation method?</h3><p>Twilight-based calculation conventions use different solar depression angles. The Muslim World League, Karachi, ISNA North America, Umm al-Qura and other methods may therefore give noticeably different dawn or night times; high-latitude nights in the UK may require further local adjustment. For method comparisons and the impact of polar conditions use the <a href="/tools/prayer-times/">Prayer Times Calculator</a> and <a href="/guides/prayer-time-methods/">full calculation methods guide</a>. The embed does not claim to know which convention your mosque uses.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="widget-deep-timezone"><h3>How location and daylight saving affect embedded Salah times</h3><p>The widget accepts a latitude, longitude and named timezone, not simply an arbitrary UTC offset. Named timezones incorporate seasonal clock changes; for example Europe/London uses different offsets in winter and summer. A visitor in another country still sees the selected mosque location's local clock. City names are descriptive labels, not geocoding proof: confirm coordinates match the label.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="widget-deep-example"><h3>Worked example: embedding a London mosque prayer timetable</h3><div class="three-tool-example"><p>Suppose an organisation wants a compact widget for London. It chooses the example coordinates 51.5072° N, 0.1276° W, Europe/London, MWL method, standard Asr convention and a dark theme. The builder generates an iframe that always loads the selected location's calculated daily times, including daylight-saving adjustments.</p><p>These settings are an illustration, <strong>not a recommendation for a named London mosque</strong>. A mosque must verify its own method, seasonal announcements and published iqamah schedule before placing a widget prominently.</p></div></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="widget-deep-privacy"><h3>Iframe security, uptime and privacy considerations</h3><p>Embeds load resources from sunnah.tools; the host site cannot guarantee they remain reachable if the service goes offline. The HTML does not collect worship history or visitor account data and does not require a sign-in. The default <code>referrerpolicy</code> reduces unnecessarily detailed cross-site referral information. Site administrators should review their own content security policy and external-embed consent requirements before installing third-party resources.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="widget-deep-mobile"><h3>Troubleshooting blank frames and mobile sizing</h3><p>If a frame is empty, verify its full HTTPS address, numerical coordinates and timezone. Some CMS editors remove iframes or block external frames by policy. Check the browser console and host platform embed permissions. Use the regular layout for a detailed card or compact layout where space is limited; do not force extremely small heights or crop the last prayer. Use the copyable widget URL to test the frame separately.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="widget-deep-comparison"><h3>Widget versus a live mosque timetable</h3><p>The widget does <strong>not</strong> retrieve an institution's verified Adhan and congregation times, prayer hall closures or local exceptions. Calculated prayer starts, actual moon-sighting events and a mosque's schedule are separate data products. For an authoritative congregation display, connect directly to the mosque's announcements instead of relabelling the calculated widget as official.</p></section>
